(      Towards Monosemanticity: Decomposing Language Model) Tj
T*
(          Representations via Sparse Dictionary Learning) Tj
T*
(                                         Ruth Sharon Raju) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(                       16:954:534 Statistical Learning for Data Science) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1     Introduction) Tj
T*
(1.1    Motivation) Tj
T*
(The future of AI safety depends on the decisions we make today. These decisions require us to) Tj
T*
(study the large language models of today in great detail. A good starting point is learning how) Tj
T*
(they internally represent meaning. The field of mechanistic interpretability research offers us this) Tj
T*
(opportunity, allowing us to break down neural networks into smaller components that are better) Tj
T*
(understood than the whole. However, their internal representations are difficult to interpret because) Tj
T*
(individual neurons are often polysemantic, responding to multiple unrelated concepts. This makes) Tj
T*
(neuron-level analysis an unreliable unit for understanding model behavior. In this project, I explore the) Tj
T*
(hypothesis that language models instead encode meaning in higher-dimensional directions of activation) Tj
T*
(space. By applying sparse dictionary learning to model activations, I aim to recover monosemantic) Tj
T*
(features that correspond to coherent concepts. This ensures a more interpretable inner view of language) Tj
T*
(models and it can further assist in debugging, alignment, and scientific insight.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1.2    Problem Statement) Tj
T*
(Let) Tj
T*
(                                              h\(l\) \(x\) in Rd                                          \(1\)) Tj
T*
(denote the activation vector at layer l for input token x. Given a corpus, we collect activations) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(                                        H = {hi }N) Tj
T*
(                                                 i=1 ,   hi in Rd .                                   \(2\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(    Since individual neurons \(coordinates of h\(l\) \(x\)\) in large language models are often polysemantic,) Tj
T*
(the problem addressed is finding a representation of the activation space that is more interpretable) Tj
T*
(than individual neurons. The goal is to obtain a higher-dimensional representation with clear semantic) Tj
T*
(interpretation.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(2     Foundations of Interpretability) Tj
T*
(Recent work on interpretability was realized through a mechanistic perspective, where neural networks) Tj
T*
(are reverse-engineered by identifying the internal computations and structures that affect model be-) Tj
T*
(havior. [1] A central challenge motivating mechanistic interpretability is the problem of superposition) Tj
T*
([2]. Neural networks have limited representational capacity relative to the number of concepts they) Tj
T*
(must encode, models often store multiple features in overlapping directions of activation space, leading) Tj
T*
(to polysemanticity. Early progress in resolving superposition came from computational structures like) Tj
T*
(induction heads, in-context learning mechanisms, and transformer circuits. [3] However, this analysis) Tj
T*
(did not by itself scale to the full diversity of features. Further analyses then revealed how compo-) Tj
T*
(sition and superposition arise naturally during training and why disentangling features is difficult in) Tj
T*
(high-dimensional systems.) Tj
T*
(    The long-term goal is to also develop scalable methods for understanding models as they grow larger) Tj
T*
(and more capable. [4] Sparse dictionary learning through sparse autoencoders is a great approach in) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
