(3     Models, Methods, and Algorithms) Tj
T*
(3.1    Base Model and Corpus) Tj
T*
(The work starts by analyzing the internal representations of a pretrained GPT-2 model. Let f denote) Tj
T*
(the transformer and let h\(l\) \(x\) in Rd denote the hidden activation vector associated with input token) Tj
T*
(x at transformer block l. Throughout, d = 512.) Tj
T*
(   For the dataset, I curated a physics-heavy corpus in order to suggest a theme to the model to make) Tj
T*
(up for the inability to implement this experiment at the original scale. The corpus consists of 82,254) Tj
T*
(non empty lines or 758,635 characters. Each line is tokenized using the GPT-2 tokenizer with padding) Tj
T*
(and truncation enabled.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3.2    Activation Extraction via Forward Hooks) Tj
T*
(The goal is to learn a feature dictionary over the activation space of a specific internal component. In) Tj
T*
(the implementation, activations are extracted from the MLP output projection of a chosen transformer) Tj
T*
(block, i.e., the module model.h[l].mlp.c proj. Concretely, for a token sequence with length L, the) Tj
T*
(hooked module produces a tensor of activations) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(                                                A in RBxLxd ,                                         \(3\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(where B is the batch size. Each valid token position is treated as one data point for dictionary learning) Tj
T*
(by flattening across batch and sequence dimensions.) Tj
T*
(    Since batches are padded, padding positions are filtered out using the attention mask. Let m in) Tj
T*
({0, 1}BxL be the attention mask; the final activation dataset is) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(                                          H = {ai }N) Tj
T*
(                                                   i=1 ,   ai in Rd ,                                 \(4\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(where N counts only positions with m = 1. To control computational cost, we cap the number of) Tj
T*
(collected token activations at a fixed budget N <= T \(with T = 400,000 in our experiments\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3.3    Token-Context Alignment for Feature Interpretation) Tj
T*
(For each retained activation vector ai , \(i\) the decoded token string and \(ii\) a short decoded context) Tj
T*
(window around that token are stored. Specifically, for a token at position t, I decoded a context) Tj
T*
(window of size +/-5 tokens:) Tj
T*
(                                   context\(t\) = decode\(xt-5:t+5 \).                                 \(5\)) Tj
T*
(This yields a list of records {\(tokeni , contexti \)}N                                       N) Tj
T*
(                                                    i=1 that is indexed identically to {ai }i=1 .) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(3.4    Sparse Autoencoder for Dictionary Learning) Tj
T*
(The sparse autoencoder maps an activation a in Rd to a latent feature vector z in Rk :) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(                                              z = ?\(We a + be \),                                     \(6\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(where We in Rkxd , be in Rk , and ? is a nonlinearity enforcing non-negativity \(ReLU in our implemen-) Tj
T*
(tation\). The decoder reconstructs the activation as) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(                                               ? = Wd z + bd ,                                      \(7\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(with Wd in Rdxk and bd in Rd . A latent space with k = 4096, corresponding to an 8x expansion) Tj
T*
(relative to d = 512, is used.) Tj
T*
